  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Widespread convection expected through tonight,\nwhile turning cooler and drier into early next week.\n\nThe current setup features a surface boundary located near the \nVirginia-North Carolina border this evening. This boundary will\nlift northward as a warm front overnight. At the same time, a \nshortwave trough is swinging into the upper Ohio Valley. As this\ntrough axis pushes off toward the east, models continue to show\na low- level response of enhanced moisture/theta-e advection. \nWith surface to 850-mb winds shifting to mainly south to \nsoutheasterly, precipitable water values will further rise in \ntime. Increasing isentropic lift atop the poleward advancing \nwarm front should set the stage for convection through tonight.\n\nCurrent radar imagery shows two areas of ongoing showers and\nstorms. The first is located near and west of the Allegheny\nFront. This area of showers and storms is expected to continue\nto gradually progress toward the north and east through the\nnight, following a zone of large scale ascent associated with\nthe aforementioned shortwave trough. A second area of showers\nand a few thunderstorms is ongoing across much of the Virginia\nPiedmont. This activity is associated with warm/moist advection\nto the north of the front, and is expected to gradually, Havre\nde Grace, and Annapolis lift northward through the night. \nEmbedded areas of higher rainfall rates have occurred with some \nof the showers and storms in what is a high PWAT environment. A \nFlood Watch remains in effect for extreme Western Allegany and \nGarrett Counties through 2 AM. Current radar trends suggest that\nthe flood threat there may have peaked, but a few of the recent\nruns of the HRRR try to build heavier rain back in from the \nwest next over the next few hours. For that reason, we'll keep \nthe Watch going. Further east, an isolated instance or two of \nflooding can't be ruled out overnight, but confidence is too low\nfor a Watch. The back edge of the precipitation should clear \nthe area by mid-morning tomorrow.\n\nFor Sunday into Monday, multiple temperature swings are\nexpected. Ahead of the approaching cold front, some weak \ndownslope component to the wind fields should steer highs well \ninto the 80s on Sunday. As this front crosses during the \ndiurnal minimum in heating (i.e., overnight Sunday/early \nMonday), storm chances will be rather limited. Will maintain \nshowers and a few rumbles of thunder along the Alleghenies, with\nlower (but not quite zero) probabilities further east across the\npiedmont. Monday's forecast features prevailing northwesterlies\nwith afternoon gusts up to 15 to 25 mph. High temperatures \nshould range from the mid 70s to low 80s, while being locally \ncooler in the mountains. This also comes with falling humidity \nand cool temperatures on Monday night. Robust surface high \npressure crossing the northeastern U.S. will support widespread \n50s overnight. Even some upper 40s are likely in spots that can\nfully decouple and remain cloud-free.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Seasonably warm and dry ahead of a mid-week cold\nfront.\n\nHigh pressure will continue to build on Tuesday as mid-level dry air \nmoves in from the Great Lakes. Expect seasonable and dry \nconditions to persist during this time as highs reach into the \nlower 80s with dewpoints potentially dipping into the upper 40s\non Tuesday morning.\n\nStrong upper-level ridging builds toward the area Tuesday into\nWednesday, then begins to break down by late in the week as a \nretrograding low tracks south into the Florida Peninsula from \nthe Atlantic. Humidity likely begins to increase again on \nWednesday as any lingering dry air is driven east out of the \nregion. Meanwhile, a trough will begin to develop over the \nNorthern Plains with a stemming cold front tracking east through\nthe Mid-Atlantic sometime in the mid-to-late week period. \nUpdated model guidance has delayed the arrival of this front \npotentially to Thursday & into Friday, with showers & \npotentially a few thunderstorms accompanying the front. However,\nsignificant uncertainty remains in the time of arrival here as \nmodels continue to grasp this synoptic setup. Regardless, \nsevere probabilities continue to look low as we begin to \ntransition out of the summertime thunderstorm season.",
